            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's definition perfectly aligns with the broad consensus of biblical interpretation and the explicit definition given by Christ in Matthew 7:26. Both the book and the common interpretations agree that a biblical fool is defined by moral disobedience (lawlessness) and a failure to act on God's word, rather than a lack of intellectual capacity. The book captures this core meaning directly with strong scriptural warrant.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A fool is one who hears God's word but fails to act on it; a person characterized by disobedience and lawlessness rather than a lack of intelligence.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Opposite: Wise\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's extensive derivation of the virgins' 'oil' as a stored record of obedience (the law lived) rather than the Holy Spirit or grace is a complex typological argument that goes beyond the basic definition of 'fool'.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The fool is a broader symbol of moral and spiritual rebellion, practical atheism, or naive gullibility, which may not always strictly require the explicit prerequisite of having 'heard God's word' before rebelling.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A person of moral and spiritual deficiency who rejects divine wisdom and lives in practical rebellion against God.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter asserts that folly is a broader category of moral deficiency and practical godlessness that does not solely consist of the specific sequence of hearing a command and then failing to do it.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Psalm 14:1 describes the fool as saying 'There is no God' in his heart, which describes practical atheism and internal corruption more broadly than the specific mechanics of hearing a word and not doing it.\",\n    \"Wisdom literature uses various distinct words for 'fool' (e.g., the naive/simple, the scoffer) that represent a spectrum of moral posture rather than a single formula.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Directly grounds the definition in Jesus' explicit contrast in Matthew 7:24-26 between the wise (hears and does) and the foolish (hears and does not).\",\n    \"Accurately distills the unanimous consensus that biblical folly is an issue of obedience (lawlessness) rather than IQ (unintelligent) into a concise, actionable standard.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Accounts for the wider semantic range of Old Testament Hebrew terms for 'fool', some of which include the simple/naive who may lack experience rather than explicitly rejecting a heard command.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Matthew 7:26\",\n    \"Psalm 14:1\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
